# Agent Directives

## Working in a repo

### Git

- Never attribute Claude or Claude Code anywhere — commit messages, GitHub
  issues/PRs, or documentation.
- Never co-author commits or PRs.
- Repos clone to `~/code/<provider>/<path>`, mirroring the URL (e.g. `~/code/github.com/rupa/z/` ← `https://github.com/rupa/z/`).

### Dependencies: use Nix

{{ if stat "/run/ostree-booted" -}}
This machine runs an **immutable Fedora Atomic** distribution (rpm-ostree based, e.g. Silverblue/Kinoite/Bluefin). The base OS image is read-only and updated atomically. Do **not** assume you can freely `dnf install` packages into the system — layering packages with `rpm-ostree install` is possible but discouraged and requires a reboot. Avoid mutating the base system.

{{ end -}}
Manage project and development dependencies with **Nix**, not system packages or `rpm-ostree` layering. This keeps the base OS clean and makes environments reproducible and per-project.

For each repository:

- Add a **`flake.nix`** defining the project's dev environment (`devShells.default`) with all needed tools and dependencies.
- Add an **`.envrc`** file so that **direnv** automatically loads the environment on `cd` into the repo. Typically:

  ```sh
  use flake
  ```

- direnv activates the flake's dev shell automatically when entering the directory, so tools are available without polluting the global system.

When setting up a new repo or adding a dependency, prefer adding it to the repo's `flake.nix` rather than installing it globally.

## Code

Before writing or reviewing code in any language, load the **`code-style`**
skill. It carries the full house style: a functional design (values and pure
functions, state at the edge) plus rigor rules adapted from JPL's Power of 10.
The rules below are the ones that hold whether or not you loaded it.

**Scope.** These govern code you write. In an existing codebase its conventions
win: match the surrounding style, and keep changes inside the scope of the
task. Don't refactor toward the house style unless asked. Where a framework
requires a shape the rules forbid, write the shape the framework requires and
keep the logic out of it. Where following a rule would make the code worse,
don't follow it — name the rule and the reason in a comment at the site.

**Non-negotiable:**

- Never discard an error. No swallowed exceptions, no silently dropped results.
  If ignoring one is correct, say why in a comment.
- Fail closed. A failed authorization check, signature verification, or parse
  denies the operation; the failure branch is never the permissive one.
- Never assemble a query, command line, path, or template by concatenating
  untrusted input. Parameterized queries, argument arrays, traversal-rejecting
  path joins, escaping by the library that owns the format.
- Secrets are read at the edge and passed inward as values. Never committed,
  never a default in code, never in a fixture or comment. A secret reaching a
  log, an error message, or a stack trace is a bug.
- Every loop that isn't iterating a finite collection — retry, poll, reconnect,
  converge — carries an explicit ceiling at the loop itself: max attempts, a
  deadline, or a cancellation signal. "It terminates in practice" is not a
  bound; a number is.
- Every effect is safe to apply twice. Retries and at-least-once delivery mean
  anything that runs once will eventually run again.
